          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A ‘Fermat theory’ is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            that extends the usual theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+rings"} commutative rings
            by permitting
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiation"} differentiation
          %p
            The term
            %em Fermat theory
            has been introduced in (
            %a{:href => "#DubucKock84"}> Dubuc-Kock 84
            ) but as the name suggests, it has its roots in an old observation of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fermat"} Fermat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \;\colon\; \mathbb{R} \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial"} polynomial
            function, then
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f (x+y) = f(x) + y \tilde{f}(x,y)
          %p
            for a unique polynomial function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f} \colon \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f}(x,y) = \frac{f(x+y) - f(x)}{y}
          %p
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \ne 0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f}(x,0) = f'(x)
          %p
            So, the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f}
            knows about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative"} derivative
            of
            = succeed "!" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
          %p
            Later
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacques+Hadamard"} Jacques Hadamard
            generalized this observation from a polynomial function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuously+differentiable+function"} continuously differentiable function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f}
            is unique if required to be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+lemma"} Hadamard lemma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+function"} differentiable function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f}
            to be continuous in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            alone.) The function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f}
            is thus called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+quotient"} Hadamard quotient
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f}
            is to be the same class of function as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
          %p
            If we take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f}(x,0) = f'(x)
            as a
            %em definition
            of the derivative, we can derive many of the basic rules for derivatives from the formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x+y) = f(x) + y \tilde{f}(x,y)
          %p
            using just algebra — no
            = succeed "!" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limits
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo +
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mi g
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f + g)' = f' + g'
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo ′
                  %mi g
                  %mo +
                  %mi f
                  %mi g
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f g)' = f' g + f g'
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi g
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f \circ g)' = (f' \circ g) g'
          %p
            These ideas continue to work if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is a smooth function from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            to
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "s," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                parameter
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/parameter"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+derivative"} partial differentiation
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The above observations suggest defining the following kind of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            %strong Fermat theory
            is an extension of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+rings"} commutative rings
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            there is a unique
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f}
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f(x + y, \vec{z}) =
                  f(x, \vec{z}) + y \tilde{f}(x,y,\vec{z})
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{z}
            is a list of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            variables which act as parameters. (Here we are abusing language by writing the operations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f}
            as if they were functions, to avoid unintuitive commutative diagrams.)
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#rings
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
            \-rings
          %p
            There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            called
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                the theory of
                = succeed "-rings" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+maps"} smooth maps
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  T(n) \coloneqq C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R})
                  \\,,
          %p
            with composition of operations defined in the obvious way. An algebra of this Lawvere theory is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+algebra"} C^∞-ring
          %p
            The theory of
            = succeed "-rings" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(M)
            is a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
            ring. More generally, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chen+space"} Chen space
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frolicher+space"} Frolicher space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(M)
            = succeed "-ring." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
          %p
            In formulas, and even more generally: for any generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
            = succeed "-ring" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi CartSp
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C^\infty(X) : \mathbb{R}^n \mapsto [CartSp^{op},Set](X,Y(\mathbb{R}^n))
          %p
            that sends each object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi CartSp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n \in CartSp
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi CartSp
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [CartSp^{op},Set]
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the presheaf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} represented
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            under the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functor"} exactness
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
          %h2#PartialDerivative Partial derivatives
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            be a Fermat theory and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            be an
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mn 1
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_1 f
          %p by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_1(x, \vec{z})  = \tilde{f}(x,0,\vec{z})
          %p
            This acts like the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+derivative"} partial derivative
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            with respect to its first argument. With a bit of more work we get a list of
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi i
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_i f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)
            denotes the set of
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_i : T(n) \to T(n)
          %p
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi i
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \le i \le n
          %p
            Now
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)
            is automatically an algebra of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            (this is true for any Lawvere theory: it is the free algebra on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            generators), whence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)
            is a commutative ring. One can check that each map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_i : T(n) \to T(n)
          %p
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivation"} derivation
            of this ring — this is really just the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+rule"} chain rule
          %h2#ModulesDerivations Modules and derivations
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            be a Fermat theory, and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            be a
            = succeed "-algebra." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is simply a module for the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            But the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivation"} derivation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta : A \to N
            of such modules depends on the
            = succeed "-structure:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            To motivate the concept, let first
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            be an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            an ordinary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta : A \to N
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo +
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta(a + b  ) = \delta(a) + \delta(b)
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi λ
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi λ
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta(\lambda a) = \lambda \delta(a)
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mi b
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta(a \cdot b) = a \delta(a) + b \delta(b)
          %p
            are equivalent to the condition that for any polynomial
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in \mathbb{R}[x_1, \cdots, x_n]
            and ring elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi p
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi n
                    %mo )
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\delta\left(
                  p(a_1, \cdots, a_n)
                  \\right)
                  \=
                  \\sum_{i= 1 }^{n}
                  \\frac{\partial p}{\partial x_i}
                  \\left(
                  a_1, \cdots, a_n
                  \\right)
                  \\delta(a_i)
                  \\,.
          %p
            (It is immediate that the first three axioms imply this one. To see the converse, apply the latter to the polynomials
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1(x,y) = x + y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi λ
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_2(x) = \lambda a
            and
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_3(x,y) = x y
          %p
            The definition of derivations for general
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %a{:href => "#PartialDerivative"} partial derivatives
            from above:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              a Fermat theory,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              a
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              an
              = succeed "-module," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %strong derivation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta : A \to N
              is a map such that for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in T(n)
              and elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_i \in A)
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo )
                    %mo =
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi n
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi n
                      %mo )
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\delta\left(
                    f(a_1, \cdots, a_n)
                    \\right)
                    \=
                    \\sum_{i= 1 }^{n}
                    \\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}
                    \\left(
                    a_1, \cdots, a_n
                    \\right)
                    \\delta(a_i)
                    \\,.
          %p
            Notice that in particular such a derivation of a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a derivation of the underlying ring. (This follows again by using the above three polynomials and remembering that by definition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)
            at least contains all polynomials.)
          %h3{:id => "K&#228;hlerDiffs"} Kähler differentials
          %p
            The sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            canonically have the structures of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p The map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo :
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi n
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d :=
                    \\langle \partial_1, \dots, \partial_n \rangle : T(n) \to \prod_{i = 1}^{n}  T(n)
            %p
              obtained from the partial derivatives is the universal
              = succeed "-derivation" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n,1)
          %p
            This means that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            is a module of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta : T(n) \to N
            is a derivation in the above sense, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            factors uniquely through the map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \partial_1, \dots, \partial_n \rangle
          %p
            The point of this theorem is that it gives us a version of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+differentials"} Kähler differentials
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)
          %p
            We may think of an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f_i) \in \prod_{i = 1}^{n}  T(n)
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+differential"} Kähler differential
            1-form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi n
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1 d x^1 + f_2 d x^2 + \cdots + f_n d x^n
            and of the derivation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d := \langle \partial_1, \dots, \partial_n \rangle
            as the operation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo :
                  %mi f
                  %mo ↦
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi i
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d  : f \mapsto \sum_i \frac{\partial f_i}{ \partial x^i} d x^i
                  \\,.
          %p
            Indeed, when the Fermat theory is that of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+algebra"} C-infinity rings
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} 1-form
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+differential+graded-commutative+superalgebras"} model structure on differential graded-commutative superalgebras
          %h2#references References
          %p The original reference is
          %ul
            %li#DubucKock84
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduardo+Dubuc"}> Eduardo Dubuc
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"} Anders Kock
              = succeed "," do
                %em On 1-form classifiers
              %strong 12
              (1984), no. 11-12, 1471–1531 (
              %a{:href => "http://home.imf.au.dk/kock/DK84.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p Parts of the above material are a summary of the following talk:
          %ul
            %li#Kock09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Kähler differentials for Fermat theories
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fields Workshop on Smooth Structures in Logic, Category Theory and Physics
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://aix1.uottawa.ca/~scpsg/Fields09/smooth.abstracts.html"} abstract
          %p For more, see:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Hoffnung"}> Alex Hoffnung
              ,
              %em Smooth Structure in Ottawa II
              (
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/05/smooth_structures_in_ottawa_ii.html#c030745"}> blog entry
              )
          %p and the comments on this blog entry.
          %p
            Refinement to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            and extension to a notion of
            %em super Fermat theory
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Carchedi"}> David Carchedi
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitry+Roytenberg"} Dmitry Roytenberg
              = succeed "," do
                %em On theories of superalgebras of differentiable functions
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1211.6134"} arxiv:1211.6134
          %p Something similar appears in def. 1.1, 1.2 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Yetter"}> David Yetter
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Models for synthetic supergeometry
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=CTGDC_1988__29_2_87_0"} NUMDAM
          %p
            For more on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+supergeometry"} synthetic differential supergeometry
          %p
            Discussion of relationship to
            %span.newWikiWord
              Cartesian differential categories
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Cartesian+differential+categories"} ?
            is here:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Simon+Lemay"}> Jean-Simon Lemay
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Cartesian Fermat Categories, a new class of Cartesian Differential Categories
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://topos.institute/events/topos-colloquium/slides/2025-06-12.pdf"} Slides
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G_EmgaM9i64"} Video
